          versions that are usually available: the vertical cover shown on the player and the original cover
          stored with the post.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most people encounter a TikTok thumbnail in the feed and assume it is the first frame. Sometimes it
          is. Often it is not. Creators add title cards, reaction faces, arrows, subtitles, and branded frames
          to make the cover readable at a glance. That is why a cover can look completely different from the
          opening second of the video. If you are collecting references for a design system, a content
          calendar, or a competitor analysis, downloading the cover is faster and cleaner than taking a
          screenshot with the interface, status bar, and recommendation buttons still visible.
        </p>
        <p>
